{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Who says of his father and of his mother, 'I have not seen him,' etc.</b> For our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Megillah 17, see there) that whoever is occupied with Torah is exempt from the commandment of honoring father and mother, and the proof is from Jacob, who was not punished for those fourteen years that he was hidden away in the house of Shem and Eber, during which he did not fulfill the commandment of honoring father and mother. This is what it says: 'who says of his father and of his mother, I have not seen him' — that he is not required to look after them, and all the more so that 'his brothers he did not recognize' — he is not required to acknowledge them, and 'his sons he did not know' — as is found in tractate Eruvin (22b): What is written, 'black as a raven'? Rava said: In whom do you find Torah? In one who makes himself cruel toward his children like a raven. As in the case of Rav Adda bar Mattena, when he would go to the house of study, his wife said to him: What shall we do for the children? He said to her: There are carobs in the marsh. This is what it says here: 'and his sons he did not know' — that even his sons he makes himself as though he does not know, and all this, why? — 'for they kept Your word and Your covenant they preserved, they taught Your judgments to Jacob,' etc. — that is to say, since they must always be occupied with Torah, therefore also 'his sons he did not know,' as aforesaid." "<b>They shall place incense before You, etc.</b> For one who occupies himself with the Torah of sacrifices is as though he offered them (Menachot 110a). 'Bless HaShem, his might (חֵילוֹ)' — his strength (כֹּחוֹ), for the Torah wears out a person's strength; therefore it says, 'Bless HaShem, his might,' that is to say, his strength. 'And the work of His hands may He accept' — that he should engage in Torah in order to keep and to do. 'He smote the loins of those who rose against him,' etc. — for if this vile enemy, the inner adversary called 'vile' (מְנֻוָּל), encounters you, drag him to the house of study (Sukkah 52b)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "The word מְנֻוָּל (mnul) appears in Sukkah 52b in the context of the evil inclination; I have rendered it 'vile' throughout to preserve the Kli Yakar's wordplay connecting it to the verse's imagery."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:37.036818261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}